</section>

<section class="home-ed-context" aria-labelledby="home-ed-context-title"><div class="home-ed-context__visual"><img src="/images/studio/permit-file-spread-v1.webp" alt="Layered property file, plans, and rural property photographs" width="1672" height="941" loading="eager" fetchpriority="low"></div><div class="home-ed-context__copy"><p class="home-ed-kicker">Context matters</p><h2 id="home-ed-context-title">What the file proves—<br>and what it doesn’t.</h2><p>A septic file can confirm what was approved, how the system was designed, and where it was intended to be. It can also reveal useful details about later changes and inspections.</p><p><strong>Current condition is not confirmed.</strong> The historical file is not a current inspection, engineering opinion, or new approval.</p><a href="/septic-records-checklist/">Read the guide to septic records <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></section>

